we turn now to a health issue many  americans can relate to nearly two years  into the pandemic covert restrictions  have kept many people indoors making it  difficult to lead an active healthy  lifestyle and that is creating problems  for doctors that extend far beyond the  virus this morning as part of our  american heart month coverage dr tara  narula introduces us to a man who's  turning his life around after a scary  life-threatening diagnosis a pulmonary  embolism  with every step benjamin henderson is on  a path to a healthier life i can't push  myself yet but my exercise is walking  i do a lot of walking right here in the  park  the 56 year old henderson is lucky to be  moving at all a little more than two  months ago he almost lost his life  i got dressed to go to the store and as  soon as this elevator came up i got on  as soon as the door closed  i went into a pant  he got himself back upstairs and called  his cousin  my cousin he helped me to the bed when i  woke up again it was 1 30 in the morning  my stomach and my chest was tight felt  like a knot so i came out and i told my  cousin i said we have to go to the  hospital so you started having symptoms  around three o'clock in the afternoon  three pm yes ma'am when did you call ems  called the mls about 1 30 in the morning  so you waited 11 hours 11 hours yes  ma'am what did that do to his chances of  survival  by not seeking care immediately he was  lucky omar letouf is henderson's  cardiovascular surgeon within 30 minutes  of meeting his patient he was ready to  operate i think if he had waited any  longer he would have died  i think he could have gone to sleep and  never woken up henderson had a pulmonary  embolism a dangerous blood clot that  often starts in the leg and travels  through the heart to the lungs a large  clot blocks blood flow causing the right  side of the heart to fail as it tries to  pump against the massive blockage in  certain cases this can lead to cardiac  arrest and possibly death  smaller emboli could be treated with  medication but in this case dr letouf  knew he had to operate and when you  opened up his pulmonary arteries and saw  the extent of the clot was it larger  than you had anticipated how does it  compare  relatives it was quite large it was  almost  16 inches long by the time we pulled the  whole thing out when you see in a cloth  that's that big what is the potential  for survival for a patient who does not  have treatment not very good  latuff says several factors put  henderson at risk including obesity and  a sedentary lifestyle henderson had  spent the better part of four years  caring for his mother who suffered from  dementia he says during that time he  ignored his own health dr la two told me  that because of lack of exercise  because of my diet because of obesity  and that's where the blood clots came  from it sounds like you're a bit of a  miracle  i feel that way  since being released from the hospital  in mid-december henderson has lost more  than 40 pounds and dr letouf says he's  encouraged by the progress what do you  think it was that saved you i'm  spiritual and my mother just passed away  seven months ago and i took care of  dude  i took care of from  almost four years five years  and um  i was a caregiver and everything  and i do believe that she was holding my  hand the whole way  you know i have a tendency scar in my  chest  that's a total reminder that uh i have  to take care of myself i have a second  chance at life  dr tara narula joins us right now so  what are some of the risk factors for  pulmonary embolism well i'm so glad you  asked because this is actually the third  leading cause of cardiovascular death  after heart attack and stroke which we  don't often talk about  and it can happen to anyone at any stage  so risk factors include things like bed  rest or long plane flights or car rides  more than four hours obesity smoking  certain hormone therapy like oral  contraceptives hormone replacement  therapy pregnancy cancer or cancer  treatments um and major hospitalization  like major prolonged hospitalizations or  major surgeries any of those things can  increase your risk and then finally  genetic conditions so some people have a  family history of clotting it's really  important to know that because you may  have a predisposition to clotting  disorders it's very scary what about  symptoms if if you're worried right so  also important because left untreated  there's a 30 mortality with pulmonary  embolism so with pulmonary embolism  we're talking about shortness of breath  chest pain that we say is pleuritic  meaning it's worse when you take a deep  breath in  some people feel a rapid heart rate and  may cough up some blood-tinged or pink  sputum and then as far as clots in the  legs we're talking about red swollen  tender painful legs usually one leg  so recognizing symptoms is really  critical and don't ignore this no please  these are these are very important to  get checked out immediately dr narula  you're the best thank you so much
 

